---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const toothCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Enamel — the armour', note: 'The body\'s hardest tissue: ~96% mineral. No cells, no repair — once enamel is lost, it stays lost.' },
    { math: '2 · Dentine — the living layer', note: '70% mineral, threaded with tubules connected to the pulp. Sensitive, and capable of slow defensive repair.' },
    { math: '3 · Pulp — the core', note: 'Nerve and blood supply in a rigid box. Inflammation has nowhere to swell — hence the severe toothache.' },
    { math: '4 · Cementum and periodontal ligament', note: 'The root\'s thin cement coating and the ligament fibres that suspend the tooth in bone — the tooth is not welded, it is hung.' },
    { math: '5 · The caries battle', note: 'Bacterial acid demineralises; saliva remineralises. Early caries is a tug-of-war, which is why fluoride and timing matter.' },
  ],
};

const phCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '6.5 - 3.5*exp(-0.25*(x-6)^2) - 4.5*exp(-2*(x-6)^2)', label: 'plaque pH after sugar', color: '#b42318', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '5.5 + 0*x', label: 'critical pH for enamel', color: '#475569', width: 1.6 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 12, label: 'hours (sugar at t = 6)' },
  y: { min: 2, max: 8, label: 'plaque pH' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/dentistry/foundational/biomedical-sciences-for-dentistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Dentistry is clinical medicine for a small, densely engineered region: the oral cavity. Its
    biomedical core is the tooth itself — four tissues arranged like a castle (enamel walls,
    dentine halls, pulp throne) — suspended in bone by a ligament, in permanent chemical warfare
    with bacterial biofilm.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the tooth as four tissues" widget="equationStep" config={toothCfg} />

  <h2>Caries is a pH battle you can watch</h2>
  <p>
    Plaque bacteria ferment sugar into acids; enamel dissolves below the critical pH of about 5.5.
    After a sugary exposure, plaque pH crashes within minutes and takes tens of minutes to
    recover — the Stephan curve. Frequent sugar means frequent crashes, and the mineral balance
    tips toward decay. Frequency matters more than quantity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the Stephan curve — plaque pH after one sugary exposure" widget="plotter" config={phCfg} />

  <h2>The mouth as a diagnostic window</h2>
  <p>
    Oral tissues report on the whole body: anaemia glosses the tongue, diabetes supercharges gum
    disease, osteoporosis thins the jaw, and some systemic diseases debut with oral signs. The
    dentist is often the first clinician to see them — the semi-annual check-up is screening, not
    vanity.
  </p>

  <h2>Periodontal disease: the slow signature</h2>
  <p>
    Below the gumline, the same biofilm drives periodontitis — inflammation destroying the
    ligament and bone that suspend teeth. It is mostly painless until late, measured in
    millimetres of probing depth and attachment loss, and managed by converting an unreachable
    biofilm into a cleanable surface. Prevention here is genuinely mechanical: disrupt the film,
    daily.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> INBDE content guide (JCNDE); Berkovitz et al., <em>Oral Anatomy, Histology and Embryology</em>; Fejerskov &amp; Kidd, <em>Dental Caries</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
